      "Dams and Lodges",
      "Beavers pile branches, mud, and stones across a stream to build a dam. The dam blocks the water and makes a pond. In the pond, the family builds a lodge, a dome of sticks and mud with a dry room inside. The entrances are underwater, which keeps out hungry animals such as wolves. The pond is also deep enough that it will not freeze to the bottom in winter, so the beavers can still swim out to their stored food.",
      "Homes for Others",
      "A beaver pond does not help only beavers. The still water and the wetlands around it become homes for fish, frogs, ducks, and insects. Scientists call beavers a keystone species, because so many other living things depend on what they build.",
      "Fast facts: Beavers are most active at dusk and at night. To warn their family of danger, they slap their flat tails on the water.",
